<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Polite Request</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Can I Have a Pencil?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain simple rules of a game or activity. Interactive: Collaborate verbally in team activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can politely explain the rules of a simple game (e.g., "Please roll the dice and move your piece."). Can collaborate with peers in a group activity, sharing ideas and listening to others (e.g., "Good idea. Let's use green!"). Can describe the inability to do something in a game or activity in a polite manner (e.g., "I'm sorry. I can't roll the dice.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a quick "Desk Inventory" activity. Students look at their desks. The teacher asks, "What do you have?" and students call out items they have (e.g., "I have a pencil!"). The teacher then asks, "What do you need?" and students might point to things they don't have. This activates vocabulary like "pencil," "paper," "desk," and "bag." Students practice saying "I have..." and "I need..." orally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a pencil and a piece of paper. The teacher says, "I have a pencil, but I don't have paper." Then the teacher turns to a puppet (or a student acting as a 'friend') and models, "Can I have paper, please?" The puppet responds, "Yes, you can!" and gives the paper. The teacher also models "I'm sorry, I can't," demonstrating the negative response. Pronunciation of /p/ in "please" and /k/ in "can't" is explicitly modeled.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using visuals and gestures.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Can I have a pencil, please?', am I asking nicely or rudely?" (Nicely)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I can't roll the dice,' can I do it?" (No)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "A Polite Request")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated dialogue on a poster or digital board, depicting two friends in a classroom setting. Dialogue genre: short, interactive conversation. Register: informal, friendly. Format: cartoon speech bubbles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Hi, friend! Can I have a pencil, please?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Yes, you can! Here you go."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "Thank you!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "My pleasure!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Later...)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student C: "Can I borrow your eraser, please?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student D: "I'm sorry, I can't. I don't have an eraser right now. I have a pencil, but I don't have an eraser."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interacting orally and conveying information, needs, and feelings in simple, direct ways. Students will listen to the dialogue, repeat after the teacher, and then identify polite requests.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the friends asking for?" (School supplies)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Student B says 'No' to the pencil." (False) "Student D has an eraser." (False)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match the requested item (pencil, eraser) with the student who has it or doesn't have it.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice making polite requests ("Can I have... please?"), politely refusing ("I'm sorry, I can't..."), and stating possession ("I have..., I don't have..."). Vocabulary: pencil, paper, desk, friend, bag, ask, need, borrow, give, can, can't, please, thank you. Pronunciation: Focus on /p/ in "please" and /k/ in "can't", blending /k-aɪ-n-d/ for "kind."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Choral Repetition & Sentence Frames):</b> The teacher uses flashcards of classroom objects (pencil, paper, book, eraser, ruler, bag). The teacher says, "Can I have a [pencil], please?" and students repeat chorally and individually. Then, the teacher prompts, "What do you say if you need a [book]?" Students respond, "Can I have a book, please?" The teacher also prompts, "What if you don't have it?" Students practice, "I'm sorry, I can't. I don't have a [book]."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Chain Drill - "I need"):</b> Students sit in a circle. The first student says, "I have a pencil." The second student says, "I don't have a pencil. Can I have a pencil, please?" The first student responds, "Yes, you can! Here you go." Then the second student says what they have, and the third student makes a request, continuing the chain. Teacher provides sentence frames on the board for support: "Can I have a _____, please?" "Yes, you can!" "I'm sorry, I can't."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Picture Card Request & Respond):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards with school supplies (pencil, paper, eraser, ruler, book, crayon). One student picks a card and asks their partner for it (e.g., "Can I have a crayon, please?"). The partner must respond, either "Yes, you can! Here you go." or "I'm sorry, I can't. I don't have a crayon." They exchange roles. Teacher circulates, listening for accurate use of target grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "A Polite Request")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play / "Classroom Helpers" game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Divide the class into small groups of 3-4 students. Each group will play a simplified board game called "School Day Helpers." The game board has squares with pictures of school supplies or actions (e.g., "lost pencil," "need paper," "help a friend," "roll the dice").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group has one die and small markers. Students take turns rolling the dice and moving their piece.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">When a student lands on a square, they must say a sentence related to it, using the target language. For example:
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If they land on "lost pencil," they say to a peer: "Can I have a pencil, please?" The peer responds appropriately ("Yes, you can!" or "I'm sorry, I can't. I don't have one.").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If they land on "help a friend," they might say: "Can I help you?" or "Do you need help?"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If they land on "roll the dice" they instruct: "Please roll the dice and move your piece."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If they cannot perform an action, e.g., if another player holds the dice and they need to roll it, they say: "I'm sorry, I can't roll the dice."</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages collaboration and polite interaction, prompting students to use "please," "thank you," and "my pleasure."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, and successful completion of the game's communicative turns. The goal is to speak naturally and politely within the game context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "School Day Helpers" game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "School Day Helpers" game, making polite requests, offering help, explaining simple rules, and politely expressing inability.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "Can I have... please?" to make requests. (Yes/No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "I'm sorry, I can't." to politely refuse or express inability. (Yes/No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (pencil, paper, friend, help, turn, roll the dice) intelligibly. (Yes/No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates verbally with peers (e.g., takes turns, listens, responds). (Yes/No)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces /p/ in "please" and /k/ in "can't" clearly. (Yes/No)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick "Exit Ticket" before leaving the class.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I ask for a pencil politely now?" (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word do you say when someone gives you something?" (______)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">6-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection Prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new polite sentence you learned today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to play the game and talk to your friends in English?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "School Day Helpers" game encouraged authentic interaction and allowed students to practice polite requests in a fun context. The use of visuals and sentence frames was highly effective for A1.1 learners.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students struggled with the "I'm sorry, I can't" phrase and defaulted to just "No." I need to provide more explicit modeling and repetitive practice for this polite refusal in the next speaking activity. Scaffolding for explaining game rules could be improved by providing pre-written phrases for them to choose from.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking and interactive requests. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on this by having students write short, polite notes or simple dialogues making similar requests, reinforcing the vocabulary and grammar used today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>